$ bash /home/work/.no-mistakes/worktrees/5f69104d19ce/01M7TFTSQXYVXHZVS34WF8J2QB/bin/fm-integration-queue.sh --help
usage: fm-integration-queue.py [-h] --project PROJECT --base BASE
                               {submit,list,claim,record-result,release,publish,resolve-accounting,landing-data} ...

Opt-in integration queue and guarded GitHub fast-forward publication.

CLI owner: this module's --help. Storage: <state>/integration-queue/<key>/,
where key hashes the canonical project path and base ref. Each SQLite database
has ready components, immutable manifests, mutable batch outcomes, accounting
progress and an event history. FULL-synchronous transactions publish each
transition atomically.
Candidate objects live beside the database, never in the project clone.

`claim --workdir <isolated-copy> -- <command> ...` holds an exclusive flock on
lease for the command's lifetime. A competing claim refuses immediately. The
command inherits that descriptor and FM_INTEGRATION_{BATCH,MANIFEST,COMMIT,
OBJECTS,LEASE_FD}; shell children retain it, even if the original claimant dies.
Call record-result/release from that shell while the descriptor is inherited.
Do not background work or close the descriptor before the integrator finishes.
There is no stale-lock timer or break-lock operation. After all holders exit,
the next claim resumes the exact durable active manifest or invalidates it if
its base, heads or candidate objects changed. Each new batch starts immediately
with up to three dependency-ordered compatible components. Conflicts and stale
heads are recorded and excluded; no auto-resolution. A component records task
IDs it depends on and is eligible only when each has landed or appears earlier
in the batch. Submission requires known dependencies and refuses cycles.
On attributed failure the batch is dropped: only the failed component requires
a new reviewed/focused-green head, while its dependents remain queued until
their dependencies can land. Across batches dependencies only wait for landing.

Submission's reviewed-head and focused-head are explicit supervisor assertions
at the exact live PR head, not test receipts or proof of permission. Owner holds
and merge authority are read by the shell adapter using existing merge helpers.
No new merge grant, review waiver, test reuse or pipeline configuration exists.
This initial implementation supports GitHub component reads only; other forge
URLs refuse explicitly. All commands emit JSON, errors go to stderr.

After running the full baseline on the candidate in the isolated integrator
copy and reviewing that exact commit, record-result binds those assertions.
Publish additionally needs a separate integration task and non-draft PR at
that exact commit, on the intended integration_branch, with all source heads
included by ancestry. The supervisor publishes the integration branch/PR via
its existing delivery path; this tool never pushes a review branch. It invokes
fm-pr-merge, which retains its authority, owner-hold, review, exact-head and
live-green guards for the integration PR and all components. Waivers and
ordinary forge merge are unavailable for a batch. Projects must be explicitly
listed in the private config/integration-queue-projects (default empty).

A normal Git push chains the original pre-push hook and rejects any advertised
old base except expected_base. Receive-pack then atomically checks that old
value while updating the single ref. No force flag, fallback or bypass exists.
PR-only protection or unreadable rules refuse, and server rejection is final.
A durable publishing intent precedes that command. After interruption, claim
proves the exact candidate is an ancestor of the remote base and every folded
PR is merged at its recorded head before idempotent merge-outcome accounting.
Accounting progress is keyed by batch, task, PR URL and exact head, with an
accounted boolean and Unix timestamp for its last transition. Publication
intent creates pending rows for all components and the integration PR.
Accounting attempts are committed before filesystem side effects; their
completion records cannot be atomic with those writes. Recovery skips completed
records even after the task's successor PR has merged. An interrupted attempt,
an earlier monitor notification, or a replaced/unreadable task identity stops
the active batch as accounting-stopped, naming the task, PR and exact head.
Repeated claim/publish and release cannot bypass that stop.

Firstmate must check the exact PR's merged head on GitHub, verify the candidate
remains in the remote base, and reconcile the prior notification and authority.
Then run the stop message's resolve-accounting command with --github-checked
and --reason describing that evidence, followed by claim again. Resolution
locks the queue, rechecks live merged-head, tree and ancestry proof, and records
one exact identity as accounted without modifying task metadata, authority
files or notifications. It does not grant merge permission or republish Git.
Coordinating merge command, monitor and queue accounting at one shared lifecycle
step remains a separate follow-up outside this change.
If the base is still expected_base the same passed candidate may retry the
existing guards; any ambiguous publication remains recoverable, never guessed
landed or silently replaced. A changed component after publication leaves
accounting unproved rather than declaring its new commits landed.
Do not release or overwrite a publishing batch until its outcome is resolved.

positional arguments:
  {submit,list,claim,record-result,release,publish,resolve-accounting,landing-data}
    submit              assert reviewed/focused-green readiness at one live
                        head
    list                read components, immutable manifests, outcomes,
                        accounting progress and recovery history
    claim               immediately prepare/resume up to three components
                        under one live lease
    record-result       record failure attribution or exact full-
                        baseline/final-review assertions
    release             requeue batch; OS lease releases when the integrator
                        command exits
    publish             land through existing merge guards; requires an opted-
                        in project and final integration PR
    resolve-accounting  manually acknowledge one stopped identity after
                        checking GitHub and prior notification/authority; then
                        claim again
    landing-data        ==SUPPRESS==

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --project PROJECT     registered canonical project clone path
  --base BASE           base branch name, e.g. main
[exit 0]

Verified observations:
Empty queue returns null without launching the integrator command
Integrator command refuses the primary project checkout
Result, release and publish all refuse calls without an inherited integrator lease
Readiness refuses mismatched review assertions and unsupported forge URLs
Real GitHub merged PR is refused despite matching exact readiness assertions
Accounting checks the real merged head and refuses an unavailable task identity
Manual resolve requires GitHub attestation and an active stopped batch
